<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Myus</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 200 BCE (approx.) · capital Myus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The smallest of the twelve Ionian cities, near the mouth of the Maeander, eventually abandoned as silt turned its harbour into marsh.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Myus was founded, by tradition, by Cydrelus, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codrus">Codrus of Athens</a>, c. 1000 BCE on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latmian-gulf">Latmian Gulf</a> near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Maeander</a>. Though small, it ranked as one of the twelve members of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ionian-league">Ionian League</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Myus lived from fishing and the farmland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Maeander</a> valley. It sent three ships to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade">Battle of Lade</a> in 494 BCE. Later the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-i">Artaxerxes I</a> granted the exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> the revenues of Myus to supply him with relish. It then paid tribute in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The silting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Maeander</a> turned the gulf into a lake and marsh, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-geographer">Pausanias</a> says mosquitoes drove the people to move to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a>, which absorbed the city by c. 200 BCE. Only a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> and scattered ruins remain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/myus.jpg" alt="Myus" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Isabeau · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Myus1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 200 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Myus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Turkey</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Myous</dd></dl></aside></div>
